    "WHAT HAPPENED: Revenue +4.7% YoY, driven by retail (+7.2%). Service revenue flat (+0.8%). Ontario outperformed network by 3.1pp on same-site growth.",
    "WHY: The 7.2pp gap between retail growth (+7.2%) and service revenue (+0.8%) points to a product mix shift — customers are spending more on retail goods relative to service time, likely reflecting either a successful product attachment push or a capacity ceiling on service appointments. Ontario's 3.1pp outperformance versus the network suggests the regional merchandising or staffing model is working — the rest of the network should be benchmarked against it. The capacity utilization heatmap (Fri–Sat saturation, Mon–Tue slack below 55%) indicates the service softness is a capacity-allocation issue, not a demand problem — demand is showing up on peak days and being turned away, while off-peak capacity sits idle.",
    "WHAT TO EXPECT: If the current trend continues, retail momentum should persist and service revenue should remain roughly flat for the next period. A specific point forecast cannot be derived from this aggregated payload.",
